Conducting a one-sample t-test
Now, let’s get to conducting our one-sample t-test. Since we are using R, the specifics will be slightly different, but we’ll still be going through the five steps of the NHST procedure.






TipActivity 1



Let’s use our one-sample t-test to evaluate whether gaze preference to the singer of the familiar song (i.e., pref1) is different than what we would expect by chance. Use a two-tailed test with an α = .05.



In plain words, what are the statistical hypotheses? What are the null and alternative hypotheses?
The next step that we usually undergo in our NHST procedure is to find the critical values. Here, we would look up the corresponding critical value in a table by hand and compare our calculated test statistic to the critical values. When computing a statistical test using software, we can usually jump to producing the p-value corresponding to our test statistic. In this case, what we are essentially doing is comparing our calculated p-value to the α level directly. In both cases, the underlying logic is the same – we set our criteria for determining significance and compare our calculated value to that.







